#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a98d925-ab88-52f8-884f-7e6b8120f7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483675} {:answer/id #uuid "3d039668-18b1-5d4a-9869-1e01eaef1bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483679} {:answer/id #uuid "589e8125-b8d8-5115-be4b-3627bde6a5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483729} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7a1cd-95f8-56b1-b089-7c0beaf8c25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484038} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c7bece-f4b8-5686-919a-a5cb331f67ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3c7bece-f4b8-5686-919a-a5cb331f67ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484057} :answer-field/id #uuid "750f6f9e-b6e0-5035-8e03-025f023dadec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50860c27-301d-5231-9268-fbee285d7c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-37146" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-3,0)$, and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/d7/d7864a36585a145e009846b23142372caeff3a68f17c9d1f7d1cf17e642f9543.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1-1)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-4)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3b25a9-06c9-5f41-a968-8e309293d9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321603} {:answer/id #uuid "482d4f1f-144b-5a7b-9de9-9c2ad029db70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321716} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d6378f-0db9-5f1e-8409-7cce5146a925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322675} {:answer/id #uuid "cefc7b47-ab4c-51d4-a2bd-04794001d2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322763} {:answer/id #uuid "e387ed9c-203e-533b-b972-2b6c0b235b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3b25a9-06c9-5f41-a968-8e309293d9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321603} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb53a28-4118-5ba5-a904-3cb2c12eb512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14add615-dec7-5e1c-992f-2e9a1bbeb58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37369" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1){e}^{0}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-1-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1){e}^{1}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e-1-2e \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,1) & =2(0){e}^{1}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =0-0-2e \\\\  & =-2e \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0){e}^{y}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2e$ at the point $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $-1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37223220-8ce2-5aaf-b13b-e51831642442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483667} {:answer/id #uuid "4141a628-787e-59cd-9b3b-4d9ed6c8303b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483690} {:answer/id #uuid "42c9840a-d25c-56c5-a13d-22ba7d99829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483691} {:answer/id #uuid "91ce1e4d-9d5f-507c-b1a8-339a62262fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483870} {:answer/id #uuid "fde67504-6547-54d3-949e-2a67417d8d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c9840a-d25c-56c5-a13d-22ba7d99829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483691} :answer-field/id #uuid "585700db-4a4c-5c97-98d7-30b257ff65a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "798ecbfd-5ca4-5e85-b538-375fc7b94df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83400" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/30/30a739bfccac0a1ccc405b7ce1fd34afd46b5a2f436c852cef0bb38a13ec1e64.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,2),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =2(0)^{2}+(2)^{2}-4(0)-2(2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)-2(0) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(0)-2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4(1)-2(1) \\\\  & =2+1-4-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),(0,2),$ and $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead01c60-0b8e-5265-95fa-6b4cdaec742d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404352} {:answer/id #uuid "db0835b3-9555-5f10-b783-ce2279912fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404353} {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} {:answer/id #uuid "23d9111e-7ce1-561b-9f4f-29ebc2c46ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404355} {:answer/id #uuid "31a2fcdf-48af-528a-bc62-4f070d949ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c3c70-3db7-55f7-8111-88c0343f9bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[4]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351746} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b8adf-77e5-5a78-94b3-8706a2ac286c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351960} {:answer/id #uuid "9eacec04-ca0d-50a6-98e3-da77f9822ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352924} {:answer/id #uuid "cf33c688-22a9-5f87-ab62-f656a7ea1b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353362} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bb70c0-87ec-593e-850c-1115f0e6beea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "148c3c70-3db7-55f7-8111-88c0343f9bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[4]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351746} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd8154f-1898-5372-9fda-b77df68be7d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e5511a5-af5b-54bb-a9ab-113418cf34fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-86725" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy-x}$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices and the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/42/42b9bcda74e29cf7012fd9576ee7bcea92bfc3f58fab4c43c854892afe3acc44.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2{e}^{(0)(0)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2{e}^{(2)(0)-(2)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2} \\\\ f(0,1) & =2{e}^{(0)(1)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,2) & =2{e}^{(0)(2)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =2{e}^{(1/2)(3/2)-(1/2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[4]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2{e}^{-2}$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2\\sqrt[4]{e}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}]}
